s supporters and  the northern newspapers there was  perhaps his finest hour  customer did the Michigan Cavalry  Brigade with great distinction for the  rest of the war participating in several  more major engagements in April 1865 he  was the first Union general to receive a  flank of truce from the rebels as their  position became hopeless he was present  at Appomattox when General Lee formally  surrendered his army effectively ending  the war in recognition for his service  to the Union victory Custer's Commander  General Philip Sheridan purchased the  table the two generals had used to sign  the articles of surrender and presented  it to Custer's new wife Elizabeth known  as Libby Custer ended the war as a major  general and a hero to the Victorious  North he was only 25 years old obviously  great things were in his future though  alsoever one-leaming problem with the  war over the Grand Army of the Republic  was being broken up its soldiers sent  home to resume their civilian lives the  peacetime Army was to be much much  smaller than the two million men must  during the war and for those  professional officers that wish to  remain in the Army well the fact was  there were simply more generals than  there were slots to employ them so  George Custer who once commanded  thousands found himself a lieutenant  colonel in command of a single Cavalry  Regiment the seventh United States  Cavalry Garrison Duty in Western forts  was a far cry from glorious Cavalry  chaunders on Civil War battlefields his  soldiers were not the idealistic  volunteers he commanded before but poor  immigrants who were often the only ones  willing to accept the low pay and dismal  conditions that the Army offered and  they all increasingly grew to dislike  Custer the man was not transitioning  well to his loss of rank and  responsibility and started taking it out  on his subordinates with random  outbursts of temper and often blurring  the lines between strict discipline and  outright cruelty his erratic Behavior  ended in 1867 when he was core martial  for being absent without leave from his  post and conduct on becoming an officer  it was suspended without pay for a year  which seems harsh until you consider  that if it had been any other officer he  would have been dismissed from the Army  entirely his War record and his powerful  friends in the high command allowed him  to get away with a slap on the wrist  it's not the last time this occurs in  his career  [Music]  in 1868 the American Indian tribes on  the Great Plains were growing  increasingly tired of the US  government's failed Promises to Keep  white settlers out of territory promised  to them by treaties tribes like the  Lakota and the Cheyenne began raiding  white settlements fighting back against  what they saw as encroachments on their  land and their way of life unfortunately  this gave the government pretext for war  against the Hostile Savages as most  whites of the time period believed them  to be that winter George Sheraton  planned an operation designed to force  the southern shared bans onto designated  reservations which would open up other  land for White Settlement the fact that  these lands were far away from the  Buffalo herds that the plains tribes  relied on was not a consent one of the  Cheyenne bands that was not yet on the  reservation was a group of about 200 led  by Chief Black Kettle survivor of the  massacre of over 150 peaceful Shan and  Arapaho Sand Creek four years earlier  despite his negative past with white  black cattle was widely known as a peace  Chief who did not want war but some of  his younger Warriors known as dog  soldiers would not be restrained and  went out on raids the seventh Cavalry  found the tracks of one of these raiding  parties and followed them back to Black  cattle's Winter encampment along the  banks of the Washita River in modern day  Western Oklahoma on November 27 1868  Custer attacked the camp in accordance  with Sheridan strategy to 