 part  of the Dakota column which left Fort  Abraham Lincoln in North Dakota and  headed Western to Montana in May 1876.  all three columns were to converge in  the area of the Bighorn Little Bighorn  rivers in southern Montana where it was  believed that the largest concentration  of Indian incumbents were they were more  right than they realized on the banks of  Little Bighorn was the largest Plains  Indian camp in living memory every  Warrior who wanted to fight the whites  had gathered in one place drawn by the  leadership of the coast  believed to be a powerful medicine man  whose Visions foretold a great Indian  victory over the whites the warriors  were a mix of Lakota Northern Shan and  Arapaho numbering between 1500 and 2000  Warriors twice as many as pre-war  government estimates Custer and the  seventh Cavalry detached from the rest  of the column to a scout ahead and they  found this Village early on the morning  of June the 25th despite being warned by  his Crow and arakara Scouts about the  size of the village Custer still  believed that he was facing no more than  800 including non-combatants like women  and children he decided not to wait for  reinforcements because he believed had  been spotted and was worried various  bands would scatter he believed the  Indians would run away when he attacked  another flawed assumption Guster split  his regiment numbering about 700 men  into battalions for the attack he  himself LED five companies to ride  around the village and attack from the  north while three companies under the  command of major Marcus Reno attacked  from the south renoating found himself  heavily engaged by far more Warriors  than he'd been inspecting and was  compelled to retreat to a Hilton top  where he was joined by three companies  led by Captain Frederick benteen who had  been similarly overwhelmed while trying  to support Custer this group found  itself surrounded fighting for its life  over the course of the next two days  before the approach of the rest of the  Dakota column compelled Sitting Bull to  disband the encampment and the various  bands headed off in different directions  it was only then on June the 27th that  his comrades found out what had happened  into Custer's group to the northeast of  where the encampment had been was a  grizzly scene blue coated bodies were  lying all over many of them scalped or  mutilated on a Hilltop known today as  Last Stand Hill George Armstrong  Custer's body was found surrounded by  the bodies of his men he'd been shot  twice in the head and in the heart every  man he led into battle that day met the  same fate over 200 in all the dead  included two of Custer's Brothers his  brother-in-law and his nephew at only 36  years old his once brilliant military  career had entered in disaster  foreign  the news of the defeat and the death of  Custer in the Battle of Little Bighorn  reached the East Coast during the  nation's Centennial celebrations and was  met with shock and Horror by the  American public Libby Custer emerged  from her morning period determined to  preserve her late husband's reputation  she believed it died a hero and deserved  to be recognized as such she blamed  major Reno and Captain Ben Dean for the  death of her husband accusing them of  cowardice a court of inquiry held in  1879 found Reno blameless for the defeat  but accusations that he was incompetent  or a coward or a drunk dogged him until  his death and many years afterwards  initially Custer was buried with his men  on the battlefield but in 1877 his body  was exhumed at least it was believed to  be his body some historians believed  they might have actually dug up the  wrong corpse and it was brought to West  point where he was reinterred with full  military honors in the West Point  Cemetery by that point the great Sioux  war was over the victory at the Little  Bighorn only delaying the inevitable  Lakota Chief Crazy Horse who had led his  Warriors to victory was compelled t